---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import BeginnerSafety from '../../../components/BeginnerSafety.astro';
import ClaimList from '../../../components/ClaimList.astro';
import SourceList from '../../../components/SourceList.astro';
import { loadGuide } from '../../../lib/guide';

const data = await loadGuide('calisthenics');
// Evidence from the getting-started section that also applies here, so it is cited rather than graded again.
const sharedIds = ['guideline-strengthening', 'strengthening-health', 'sex-similar', 'pushup-scaling'];
const shared = sharedIds.map(id => data.claimById.get(id)!);
const everyClaim = [...data.claims, ...shared];
const sourceIds = new Set(everyClaim.flatMap(claim => claim.data.sources.map(source => source.id)));
const sources = [...sourceIds].map(id => data.sourceById.get(id)!);
const gaps = [
  'No systematic review of calisthenics or bodyweight training for beginners was found when searching Europe PMC. The evidence here is extrapolated from resistance-training trials, a few push-up trials and muscle-activity studies, mostly in young men.',
  'The L-sit, wall handstand, planche lean and tuck front lever have no training-outcome evidence in what was found. Handstand papers describe balance control in gymnasts, not how a beginner should train it. The steps and gates for these skills are coaching conventions.',
  'The one study of women learning the pull-up described no control group and combined strength and aerobic work, so it cannot show what causes a first pull-up.',
  'No source was found on wrist, elbow or shoulder injury rates in calisthenics, so none are stated. The wrist claims come from patients already seeking care, a self-selected survey of adult handstand practitioners, an under-18 review and laboratory studies.',
  'No review of what keeps beginners training was found that fits home bodyweight exercise. Two general meta-analyses (the intention-behaviour gap in physical activity, and dropout from interval training) are cited as indirect evidence, so the plan length, session size and missed-session rules are conventions.',
  'Unresolved, not graded: four sources were read by abstract only and need their full text before a claim is made. They are a 2018 meta-analysis of plans that state when and where to exercise (Silva and colleagues, doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0206294), which does not say in its abstract what the reinforcement was; a 2025 study linking bench press and weighted push-up strength in 11 trained men (van den Tillaar and colleagues, doi:10.1519/jsc.0000000000005160), which does not say how the push-up load was defined; a 2025 trial of pull-up routines in 30 inactive men (Sepehri Rahnama and colleagues, doi:10.3390/sports13120433), which does not describe the routine; and a 2025 muscle-activity study of the exercises used to prepare for the planche (Rosaci and colleagues, doi:10.1519/jsc.0000000000005074), which does not name the exercises. None is in the source list, and none is relied on.',
  'No validated readiness screen for handstand work was found, and no prevention trial for wrist pain. The one adult wrist source is a self-selected survey of handstand practitioners, so it gives no risk for beginners.',
  'The placement table on the lifters page, the fast-start week, the minimum session and the missed-session rules are coaching conventions with no source.',
  'The side-plank hold times (start at 20 seconds a side, add about 5 seconds a week, move on at 30 seconds) and the glute-bridge foot position (heels about 25 to 30 centimetres from the hips) are coaching conventions and practical starting points with no source. Hold times are progression cues, not safety limits.',
  'The plan has not been reviewed by a physiotherapist or a strength coach, and stays labelled draft until it is.',
];
---
<Base section="calisthenics" title="Evidence" description="The graded claims and sources behind the calisthenics guide, with each claim's limits and the gaps in the evidence.">
  <Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: 'Calisthenics', href: '/calisthenics/' }, { label: 'Evidence' }]} />
  <section class="hero page-head">
    <p class="eyebrow">Evidence</p>
    <h1>What this guide rests on</h1>
    <p class="lede">Each claim has a grade, its limits and its sources. The grades follow the <a href="/strength/evidence/">strength section's rubric</a>. Every journal source had its abstract read and its DOI, title, year and retraction status checked on Crossref. That check shows the paper exists; it does not show that the claim fits perfectly here, so the limits are stated.</p>
  </section>

  <BeginnerSafety />

  <section aria-labelledby="gaps">
    <h2 id="gaps">What the evidence does not cover</h2>
    <ul class="prose">{gaps.map(gap => <li>{gap}</li>)}</ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="claims">
    <h2 id="claims">Claims and grades</h2>
    <ClaimList claims={data.claims.map(claim => claim.id)} claimById={data.claimById} sourceById={data.sourceById} sourcesHref="" />
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="shared">
    <h2 id="shared">Also relevant, from getting started</h2>
    <p class="prose">These claims were graded for the <a href="/getting-started/evidence/">getting-started evidence page</a>, where their sources and limits are listed in full. They apply here too.</p>
    <ClaimList claims={sharedIds} claimById={data.claimById} sourceById={data.sourceById} sourcesHref="" />
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="sources">
    <h2 id="sources">All sources</h2>
    <SourceList sources={sources} claims={everyClaim} evidenceHref="/calisthenics/evidence/" />
  </section>
</Base>
